   Nethack heavily employs the "+=" and "-=" operators, which
didn't show up in C until almost the days of K&R1: 1977, or
thereabouts. Therefore, Nethack cannot possibly be compiled
on pre-C compilers written before 1977 or so.

   Variable declarations, on the other hand, have been around
in C since its creation. Certainly C compilers had diagnostics
for undefined variables by 1975. (References: cman.pdf, ctut.pdf)
You might, perhaps, be thinking of B, in which identifiers
not declared as variables were treated as labels... until the compiler
reached the end of a function without seeing a label definition, at
which point it would error out. (Reference: kbman.html) Well, okay,
the B compiler required variable definitions, too.

   If you're not thinking of Perl, maybe you're thinking of Fortran.

